No answer leak detected. The problem is a clean statement. Here is the proof.

---

**Claim:** Yes, such a δ > 0 exists.

**Proof.** Let $S^1 = \{(x,y)\in\mathbb{R}^2 : x^2+y^2=1\}$ and let $\{U_\alpha\}$ be an open cover of $S^1$ (each $U_\alpha$ open in $\mathbb{R}^2$).

For each $p \in S^1$, since $\{U_\alpha\}$ covers $S^1$, there is some $\alpha(p)$ with $p \in U_{\alpha(p)}$. As $U_{\alpha(p)}$ is open in $\mathbb{R}^2$, there exists $r > 0$ with the Euclidean ball $B(p,r) \subseteq U_{\alpha(p)}$. Define

$$g(p) = \sup\{\, r > 0 : B(p,r) \subseteq U_\alpha \text{ for some } \alpha \,\}.$$

Then $g(p) > 0$ for every $p \in S^1$.

**$g$ is lower semicontinuous.** Suppose $g(p) > a$. Then there is $\alpha$ and $r > a$ with $B(p,r) \subseteq U_\alpha$. For any $q$ with $d(p,q) < r - a$, the triangle inequality gives $B(q, a) \subseteq B(p, r) \subseteq U_\alpha$, hence $g(q) \ge a$. In particular $g(q) > a$ for all $q$ in a neighborhood of $p$. So $\{p : g(p) > a\}$ is open for every $a$; $g$ is lower semicontinuous.

**The infimum of $g$ on $S^1$ is positive.** The sets $\{p \in S^1 : g(p) > 1/n\}$ are open in $S^1$ (relative topology) and their union is all of $S^1$, since $g > 0$ everywhere. By compactness of $S^1$, finitely many cover $S^1$; taking $N = \max$ of the corresponding indices gives $g(p) \ge 1/N$ for all $p \in S^1$. Set

$$m = \inf_{p \in S^1} g(p) \ge \frac{1}{N} > 0.$$

(Equivalently, a lower semicontinuous function on a compact set attains its minimum, and since $g>0$ everywhere the minimum is positive.)

**Constructing $\delta$.** Choose $\delta = m/2 > 0$. Let $q$ be any point in the annulus

$$A_\delta = \{(x,y) : 1-\delta < \sqrt{x^2+y^2} < 1+\delta\}.$$

Let $\rho = \sqrt{x^2+y^2} = |q| \in (1-\delta,\, 1+\delta)$ and let $p = q/|q| \in S^1$ be the radial projection of $q$ onto the unit circle. The Euclidean distance is

$$d(q, p) = \bigl|\,|q| - 1\,\bigr| = |\rho - 1| < \delta < m.$$

By definition of $m \le g(p)$, there exists $\alpha$ with $B(p, m) \subseteq U_\alpha$. Since $d(q,p) < m$, we have $q \in B(p,m) \subseteq U_\alpha$.

Thus every point of $A_\delta$ lies in some $U_\alpha$, i.e. $\{U_\alpha\}$ covers $A_\delta$.

Therefore such a $\delta > 0$ exists. $\blacksquare$

---

**Remark.** The key ingredients are: (1) each $U_\alpha$ is open in the *ambient* $\mathbb{R}^2$, so it contains a full 2-dimensional ball around each of its points on $S^1$ (not merely a relative neighborhood in $S^1$); (2) compactness of $S^1$ lets us extract a uniform positive "thickness" $m$ from the pointwise data. The radial projection $q \mapsto q/|q|$ is the natural way to transfer coverage from $S^1$ to a nearby annulus, since the nearest point of $S^1$ to any $q \neq 0$ is exactly $q/|q|$.

### PROOF COMPLETE
